{"name":"Orange Squash","hex":"#c27635","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"orange squash","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sweetened drink, made with oranges but often containing additional flavourings, that is served diluted with water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange_squash"}],"entries":[{"title":"Squash (drink)","blocks":[{"text":"Squash is a non-alcoholic beverage with syrup used in beverage making. It is usually fruit-flavoured, made from fruit juice, water, and sugar or a sugar substitute. Modern squashes may also contain food colouring and additional flavouring. Some traditional squashes contain herbal extracts, most notably elderflower and ginger."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Garcinia_indica_yellow_and_red_syrups_and_drinks.jpg/330px-Garcinia_indica_yellow_and_red_syrups_and_drinks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Squash_(drink)","wikidata":"Q1190923"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/ISS_Expedition_5_crew.jpg/500px-ISS_Expedition_5_crew.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm